# Backend C# style

Target **.NET 10** / current C#. Prefer one clear DI shape per type. Do not keep parallel “legacy” APIs that DI or callers can still bind.

## Primary constructors (required for new / touched types)

Use C# primary constructors for DI and immutable collaborators. Prefer assigning only what must escape the parameter list; avoid duplicating the same fields in a classic ctor body.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD
public sealed class ConversationMetricsRecorder(
    IConversationTurnMetricRepository turnMetricRepository,
    IClock clock,
    IEffectiveSettingsAccessor effectiveSettings,
    IOptionsMonitor<MetricsOptions> options) : IConversationMetricsRecorder
{
    public bool IsEnabled =>
        effectiveSettings.IsSet
            ? effectiveSettings.Current.MetricsEnabled
            : options.CurrentValue.Enabled;
}

// ❌ BAD — classic ctor + private fields when a primary ctor fits
public sealed class ConversationMetricsRecorder
{
    private readonly IOptionsMonitor<MetricsOptions> _options;
    public ConversationMetricsRecorder(IOptionsMonitor<MetricsOptions> options) => _options = options;
}
```

When editing an existing classic-ctor type, migrate it to a primary constructor in the same change if the touch is non-trivial.

## No legacy implementation surfaces

Do **not** add or keep parallel public constructors, wrappers, or “compat” overloads that duplicate the primary DI shape (e.g. `IOptions<T>` beside `IOptionsMonitor<T>` + accessor). MS DI validates **all public** constructors and fails on ambiguity.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — second public ctor DI can also satisfy
public ConversationMetricsRecorder(..., IEffectiveSettingsAccessor a, IOptionsMonitor<MetricsOptions> m) { }
public ConversationMetricsRecorder(..., IOptions<MetricsOptions> options) { } // legacy

// ✅ GOOD — single public DI ctor (primary). Test helpers: internal factory / InternalsVisibleTo, or construct with mocks of the real deps
```

- Prefer updating tests to the primary signature over shipping legacy public overloads
- `internal` test-only helpers are allowed; they must not be public DI entry points
- Do not reintroduce `IOptions<T>` capture-at-ctor on hot paths that already use `IOptionsMonitor<T>` / sticky accessors

## Latest C# syntax (prefer on touch)

| Prefer | Avoid |
| --- | --- |
| Primary constructors | Classic ctor + boilerplate fields when equivalent |
| `required` / init where appropriate | Optional mutable public set-only bags without need |
| Collection expressions `[..]`, `[]` | `new List<T> { }` when a collection expression is enough |
| Pattern matching / switch expressions | Nested if-else for the same shape |
| `is null` / `is not null` | `== null` / `!= null` |
| File-scoped namespaces | Block-scoped namespaces on new files |
| Target-typed `new()` | Redundant `new TypeName()` when the type is obvious |
| Raw string literals `"""` for multi-line text | Heavy escaped `"\n"` concatenation |

Generated code (EF migrations, etc.) is exempt. Do not churn untouched files solely for syntax fashion.

## Do

- One public construction path for DI-registered types
- `IOptionsMonitor<T>` (or sticky accessor) for options that must hot-reload or honor effective settings
- Match existing layer folder patterns (Application / Domain / Infrastructure / hosts)

## Do not

- Public dual ctors “for tests” or “for migration”
- Mark dual ctors with `[ActivatorUtilitiesConstructor]` as a substitute for removing ambiguity — prefer a single public shape
- Capture `IOptions<T>.Value` in a field on types that must see overlay / sticky updates
